            "captions": {
                "linkedin": "What if a simple phone\u2011free morning could quiet a toddler\u2019s racing mind before the school bus even arrives?  \n\nThe daily commute feels like a race against time, a high\u2011stakes moment that spikes stress for every family member.  \n\nWhen a nearby school tried a phone\u2011free start, the change was immediate for my two\u2011year\u2011old daughter. She no longer fidgeted with a glowing screen; instead, she settled into the rhythm of the ride, eyes on the road, imagination humming.  \n\nIn that quiet, the phone became a tool, not a distraction. She could still enjoy her favorite story-just not on a screen. The absence of a device let her focus on the narrative itself, on the cadence of words, on the world outside the car window.  \n\nResearch from the American Academy of Pediatrics notes that limiting screen exposure in early childhood supports attention development and emotional regulation. The school\u2019s experiment echoed that finding: a brief, intentional break from screens can lower anxiety and improve presence.  \n\nFor families, the lesson is simple yet profound: the smallest adjustments-like a phone\u2011free morning-can reshape how children experience the world, turning a frantic commute into a moment of calm connection.\n\n#DigitalGuardian #DigitalParenting #DigitalSafety #ParentingTips",
                "bluesky": "When a school went phone\u2011free for the morning, my 2\u2011year\u2011old\u2019s panic vanished-she read her story without staring at a screen. Turns out, ditching the glow turns a gadget into a simple tool, not a stress trigger.\n\nWant to read more?... check out my LinkedIn"
